# security-audit.denylist — names that must not be visible in the shipping binary.
#
# Copy this to  security-audit.denylist  (next to your .xcodeproj, i.e. $SRCROOT) or point
# $SECURITY_AUDIT_DENYLIST at it. One extended-regex (ERE) per line. '#' starts a comment.
# Matching is against every string in the binary, so anchor loosely and keep entries specific.
#
# Remember what a build-phase gate can and cannot do:
#   • It CATCHES a name that reached the binary. It does not rename anything.
#   • Only list names that are NOT resolved by string at runtime. If a class is loaded via a
#     storyboard/XIB, Codable, KVC, Core Data, NSClassFromString or #selector, renaming it at the
#     source to satisfy this gate will break the app. Audit those names out some other way.

# ── Your sensitive class names (the class-dump finding) ───────────────────────────────────
# LicenseValidator
# JailbreakDetector
# RootBeerish
# AntiTamper(ing)?Manager

# ── Internal hosts / endpoints that should never be literals in a binary ──────────────────
# internal\.example\.com
# staging-api\.

# ── Framework/SDK tells you would rather not advertise ────────────────────────────────────
# YourSecretSDK
